1
00:00:05,475 --> 00:00:09,825
This is relflow, a Python library that builds PyTorch models straight from

2
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:11,262
nested records.

3
00:00:11,262 --> 00:00:15,625
In the next half hour, we'll take it apart, piece by piece, and watch every

4
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:17,908
piece work.

5
00:00:17,908 --> 00:00:21,696
Here's one customer of an online store, written as YAML.

6
00:00:21,696 --> 00:00:24,596
They've been a customer for 420 days.

7
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:28,846
They've placed orders, and every order has its own list of line items:

8
00:00:28,846 --> 00:00:31,946
a product SKU, and how many were bought.

9
00:00:31,946 --> 00:00:34,758
The last two fields are what we'd like to predict:

10
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:39,808
did this customer leave the next month, and how much did they spend?

11
00:00:39,975 --> 00:00:41,788
The usual move is to flatten it:

12
00:00:41,788 --> 00:00:45,163
one row per customer, one column per hand-made feature.

13
00:00:45,163 --> 00:00:46,313
Count the orders.

14
00:00:46,313 --> 00:00:47,388
Count the items.

15
00:00:47,388 --> 00:00:48,938
Average the quantities.

16
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:50,550
Count the distinct products.

17
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:53,013
But every column throws something away.

18
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:56,350
Once the quantities are averaged, you can no longer ask which product was

19
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:59,925
bought five at a time, or whether it was in the newest order.

20
00:00:59,925 --> 00:01:03,463
Which value belongs to which item, and which item to which order:

21
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:06,475
that structure is gone.

22
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:08,771
relflow takes the opposite road.

23
00:01:08,771 --> 00:01:12,633
You describe the record's shape once, in Python, and that description is the

24
00:01:12,633 --> 00:01:13,871
architecture.

25
00:01:13,871 --> 00:01:17,796
Keywords name the fields, and a type says what each value means.

26
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:22,483
A Branch marks a repeated collection, with room for 64 orders, each with up

27
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:24,208
to 16 line items.

28
00:01:24,208 --> 00:01:26,821
And mask equals True marks an answer:

29
00:01:26,821 --> 00:01:31,858
a value the model will learn to predict, but never gets to see.

30
00:01:32,008 --> 00:01:34,796
From that tree, relflow builds the network.

31
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:38,708
Every input field gets a small embedding module that turns its values into

32
00:01:38,708 --> 00:01:39,771
vectors.

33
00:01:39,771 --> 00:01:44,071
Every branch, and the root, gets its own transformer encoder, which reads

34
00:01:44,071 --> 00:01:47,208
a collection and passes a summary up to its parent.

35
00:01:47,208 --> 00:01:51,496
And every target gets a decoder, which reads the summaries back down the tree

36
00:01:51,496 --> 00:01:53,083
to make its prediction.

37
00:01:53,083 --> 00:01:57,283
With the medium preset, this one schema becomes a network of twelve million

38
00:01:57,283 --> 00:01:59,758
parameters.

39
00:02:00,383 --> 00:02:03,621
We'll follow one batch of records through that whole machine.

40
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:07,946
How nested Arrow data becomes rectangles of tensors, and why a null is not

41
00:02:07,946 --> 00:02:09,046
a zero.

42
00:02:09,046 --> 00:02:13,358
How a number becomes a vector, how a vocabulary grows while it trains, and

43
00:02:13,358 --> 00:02:16,921
how a hash can learn equality without memorizing names.

44
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:20,671
What happens inside a branch, and why padding costs nothing.

45
00:02:20,671 --> 00:02:25,108
Why softmax attention can't count, and the small trick relflow uses to fix

46
00:02:25,108 --> 00:02:26,183
that.

47
00:02:26,183 --> 00:02:30,758
And finally, how a decoder asks the tree a question, and a real model learning

48
00:02:30,758 --> 00:02:31,946
to answer it.

49
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:36,808
Every grid, curve and number in this video comes from running relflow itself,

50
00:02:36,808 --> 00:02:38,583
on the computer that rendered it.

51
00:02:43,665 --> 00:02:45,678
Let's look closely at that tree.

52
00:02:45,678 --> 00:02:47,465
Every node has an address:

53
00:02:47,465 --> 00:02:51,028
a path that starts at the root, which is written as a single slash.

54
00:02:51,028 --> 00:02:55,128
The root is anonymous, and it always holds exactly one observation.

55
00:02:55,128 --> 00:02:57,702
Anything repeated lives in a branch beneath it.

56
00:02:57,702 --> 00:03:03,115
So the SKU of a line item lives at slash orders, slash line items, slash SKU.

57
00:03:03,115 --> 00:03:05,740
Addresses are how relflow names everything:

58
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:10,090
predictions, metrics, exported embeddings, and even edits to a model that's

59
00:03:10,090 --> 00:03:12,690
already trained.

60
00:03:12,865 --> 00:03:15,003
Each branch also fixes a shape.

61
00:03:15,003 --> 00:03:19,353
Orders holds up to 64 entries, and each order up to 16 line items.

62
00:03:19,353 --> 00:03:22,040
So the tensor for SKU always has the shape:

63
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:27,065
batch, by one root, by 64, by 16, however many items a real customer happens

64
00:03:27,065 --> 00:03:28,078
to have.

65
00:03:28,078 --> 00:03:29,715
Short lists get padded.

66
00:03:29,715 --> 00:03:33,853
Long lists get cut, keeping either the first entries, or the last ones, which

67
00:03:33,853 --> 00:03:37,065
is what you want for a history sorted oldest to newest.

68
00:03:37,065 --> 00:03:42,165
The shapes are fixed by the schema before a single record arrives.

69
00:03:42,357 --> 00:03:43,544
Now the modules.

70
00:03:43,544 --> 00:03:47,382
A leaf owns an embedder, which turns its values into vectors of one shared

71
00:03:47,382 --> 00:03:49,107
width, called d model.

72
00:03:49,107 --> 00:03:53,769
If the leaf is a target, or if you ask for its embedding, it also owns a decoder.

73
00:03:53,769 --> 00:03:56,407
A branch owns an encoder in three parts.

74
00:03:56,407 --> 00:04:00,544
First, a layer that lets the fields of the same item talk to each other.

75
00:04:00,544 --> 00:04:04,607
Then a stack of transformer layers that runs across the whole collection.

76
00:04:04,607 --> 00:04:08,507
And finally a reduction, which boils the collection down to a few vectors

77
00:04:08,507 --> 00:04:11,032
for its parent.

78
00:04:11,173 --> 00:04:14,723
Here's where the twelve million parameters of our customer model actually

79
00:04:14,723 --> 00:04:15,611
live.

80
00:04:15,611 --> 00:04:19,811
Almost all of them are in the three encoders, the root's being the deepest,

81
00:04:19,811 --> 00:04:21,686
and in the two decoders.

82
00:04:21,686 --> 00:04:23,373
The embedders are tiny.

83
00:04:23,373 --> 00:04:28,073
And the SKU embedder is the smallest of all, because its vocabulary is still

84
00:04:28,073 --> 00:04:28,998
empty.

85
00:04:28,998 --> 00:04:33,423
It will grow as labels arrive, while it trains.

86
00:04:33,598 --> 00:04:35,823
Those sizes come from a preset.

87
00:04:35,823 --> 00:04:39,786
relflow ships five, from extra small to extra large.

88
00:04:39,786 --> 00:04:43,861
Each one sets the width, the depth of the root and of every branch, the number

89
00:04:43,861 --> 00:04:47,998
of attention heads, and how many summary vectors each branch passes to its

90
00:04:47,998 --> 00:04:48,998
parent.

91
00:04:48,998 --> 00:04:51,336
Our customer model used medium.

92
00:04:51,336 --> 00:04:55,173
Anything you set yourself wins, but only at the node where you set it.

93
00:05:00,005 --> 00:05:01,668
Let's follow a real batch.

94
00:05:01,668 --> 00:05:03,043
Here are four orders:

95
00:05:03,043 --> 00:05:08,497
two items, one item with a missing quantity, no items at all, and five items.

96
00:05:08,497 --> 00:05:12,409
In memory, Apache Arrow doesn't store lists the way Python does.

97
00:05:12,409 --> 00:05:15,997
Every item of every order goes into one long column per field:

98
00:05:15,997 --> 00:05:19,734
all the SKUs, back to back, and all the quantities.

99
00:05:19,734 --> 00:05:23,759
A separate offsets array says where each order's items start and stop.

100
00:05:23,759 --> 00:05:28,797
Order zero owns items zero and one, order one owns item two, order two starts

101
00:05:28,797 --> 00:05:33,872
and stops at three, so it's empty, and order three owns the last five.

102
00:05:33,872 --> 00:05:35,959
The model, though, wants rectangles:

103
00:05:35,959 --> 00:05:39,059
in this small example, a grid of four slots per order.

104
00:05:39,059 --> 00:05:42,784
So for every item it keeps, relflow computes a single number, its placement

105
00:05:42,784 --> 00:05:43,797
in the grid:

106
00:05:43,797 --> 00:05:47,334
the parent's position, times the branch length, plus the item's slot within

107
00:05:47,334 --> 00:05:48,634
its own list.

108
00:05:48,634 --> 00:05:51,734
Item four, for instance, is order three's second item:

109
00:05:51,734 --> 00:05:54,834
three times four, plus one, is thirteen.

110
00:05:54,834 --> 00:05:59,472
Then every value is scattered to its placement in one vectorized step.

111
00:05:59,472 --> 00:06:03,259
Order two is empty, so its whole row stays padding.

112
00:06:03,259 --> 00:06:06,709
Order three has five items but only four slots.

113
00:06:06,709 --> 00:06:11,722
By default relflow keeps the head, the first four, so F11 is dropped.

114
00:06:11,722 --> 00:06:15,834
With overflow equals tail it keeps the last four instead, which is what you

115
00:06:15,834 --> 00:06:19,388
want for a history that's sorted oldest to newest.

116
00:06:19,388 --> 00:06:22,101
Nested branches simply repeat the rule.

117
00:06:22,101 --> 00:06:26,238
In the customer model, an order's own placement becomes the parent position

118
00:06:26,238 --> 00:06:31,238
for its line items, so every item lands somewhere in a grid of 64 orders by

119
00:06:31,238 --> 00:06:32,638
16 items.

120
00:06:32,638 --> 00:06:35,988
And all of it is NumPy over Arrow's buffers:

121
00:06:35,988 --> 00:06:40,713
no Python loop ever walks the records one at a time.

122
00:06:40,872 --> 00:06:44,509
Alongside the values, every slot also gets a state.

123
00:06:44,509 --> 00:06:48,222
Here's the real state grid relflow produced for the quantities of these four

124
00:06:48,222 --> 00:06:49,297
orders.

125
00:06:49,297 --> 00:06:53,797
V for a real value, N for the quantity that was explicitly null, and P for

126
00:06:53,797 --> 00:06:56,322
padding, where there was no item at all.

127
00:06:56,322 --> 00:07:00,547
And the SKUs came out as small integers, looked up in a dictionary that's

128
00:07:00,547 --> 00:07:01,972
local to this batch.

129
00:07:01,972 --> 00:07:05,222
We'll see later how those meet the model's own vocabulary.

130
00:07:10,195 --> 00:07:12,345
Why does relflow track a state at all?

131
00:07:12,345 --> 00:07:15,908
Because inside a tensor, a zero is just a zero.

132
00:07:15,908 --> 00:07:17,083
Take a measurement:

133
00:07:17,083 --> 00:07:21,245
one sensor read exactly zero, and another never reported.

134
00:07:21,245 --> 00:07:25,733
Fill the null with zero, as so many pipelines do, and these two records become

135
00:07:25,733 --> 00:07:30,170
identical, even though they need different answers.

136
00:07:30,328 --> 00:07:33,553
So relflow keeps two separate inputs for every slot.

137
00:07:33,553 --> 00:07:34,703
One is the content:

138
00:07:34,703 --> 00:07:36,703
the number, the label, the hash.

139
00:07:36,703 --> 00:07:39,216
The other is a state, one of five tokens:

140
00:07:39,216 --> 00:07:43,691
valued, null, padded, masked, and a fifth reserved for extensions.

141
00:07:43,691 --> 00:07:46,166
Each state has its own learned vector.

142
00:07:46,166 --> 00:07:49,941
A field's embedder computes a vector from the content, and combines it with

143
00:07:49,941 --> 00:07:51,516
the vector for the state.

144
00:07:51,516 --> 00:07:56,503
So a zero never has to stand in for null, or for padding.

145
00:07:56,662 --> 00:07:59,824
relflow's own test suite checks exactly this.

146
00:07:59,824 --> 00:08:04,862
In proof P038, every measurement that's present is exactly zero, and missingness

147
00:08:04,862 --> 00:08:06,774
alone decides the label.

148
00:08:06,774 --> 00:08:09,337
The model separates the two classes perfectly:

149
00:08:09,337 --> 00:08:11,087
an AUC of one.

150
00:08:11,087 --> 00:08:15,662
Fill the nulls with zeros before training, and the AUC collapses to one half,

151
00:08:15,662 --> 00:08:16,849
a coin flip.

152
00:08:16,849 --> 00:08:19,937
A control with random nulls lands at chance too.

153
00:08:19,937 --> 00:08:24,637
The information was destroyed before the model ever saw it.

154
00:08:24,845 --> 00:08:27,008
Null and padding are different, too.

155
00:08:27,008 --> 00:08:28,770
A null is a real coordinate:

156
00:08:28,770 --> 00:08:32,933
the item exists, its quantity is null, and attention can see that.

157
00:08:32,933 --> 00:08:35,158
Padding is no coordinate at all.

158
00:08:35,158 --> 00:08:39,633
Padded slots are zeroed, and they're excluded from every attention computation

159
00:08:39,633 --> 00:08:41,133
and every average.

160
00:08:41,133 --> 00:08:42,470
Here's the mask:

161
00:08:42,470 --> 00:08:46,533
live tokens attend to each other, and padding never takes part, however much

162
00:08:46,533 --> 00:08:49,620
room the branch reserves.

163
00:08:49,770 --> 00:08:52,083
A few more cases, to pin it down.

164
00:08:52,083 --> 00:08:53,770
An empty list is valued:

165
00:08:53,770 --> 00:08:55,608
there is a list, and it's empty.

166
00:08:55,608 --> 00:08:57,308
A null list is not.

167
00:08:57,308 --> 00:09:00,383
A label the model has never seen is still valued;

168
00:09:00,383 --> 00:09:02,720
only its content is unknown.

169
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:06,520
And a value we hide on purpose gets the fourth state, masked, which is how

170
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,620
targets work.

171
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:08,495
We'll get there.

172
00:09:13,335 --> 00:09:14,473
Start with one number:

173
00:09:14,473 --> 00:09:16,935
a price, say, nineteen ninety-nine.

174
00:09:16,935 --> 00:09:20,473
Networks like their inputs near zero, with a spread of about one, so the first

175
00:09:20,473 --> 00:09:22,173
step is to standardize:

176
00:09:22,173 --> 00:09:25,560
subtract a mean, divide by a standard deviation.

177
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:27,460
But relflow streams its data.

178
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:29,885
It never holds the whole column at once.

179
00:09:29,885 --> 00:09:32,998
So each training batch is boiled down to three numbers:

180
00:09:32,998 --> 00:09:35,685
how many values, their mean, and their variance.

181
00:09:35,685 --> 00:09:38,610
And summaries like these merge exactly.

182
00:09:38,610 --> 00:09:42,798
The new mean moves toward the batch's mean in proportion to its size, and

183
00:09:42,798 --> 00:09:46,948
the variance gets a correction for how far apart the two means were.

184
00:09:46,948 --> 00:09:51,685
After six batches, the running statistics match the exact mean and variance

185
00:09:51,685 --> 00:09:56,735
of every value seen, without keeping a single one of them.

186
00:09:56,935 --> 00:10:00,098
Now the standardized value, z, becomes a vector.

187
00:10:00,098 --> 00:10:04,623
relflow evaluates sines and cosines of z at thirteen frequencies, each double

188
00:10:04,623 --> 00:10:08,735
the last, from pi over 256 up to sixteen pi.

189
00:10:08,735 --> 00:10:10,510
The slow ones change gently:

190
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:12,460
they say roughly where z is.

191
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:14,885
The fast ones wiggle many times per unit:

192
00:10:14,885 --> 00:10:17,423
they tell apart values that are very close.

193
00:10:17,423 --> 00:10:21,323
Read them all off at one value of z, and you get twenty-six numbers:

194
00:10:21,323 --> 00:10:25,185
a fingerprint that's different for every value.

195
00:10:25,318 --> 00:10:26,131
Why bother?

196
00:10:26,131 --> 00:10:28,656
Suppose the effect you need to learn is narrow:

197
00:10:28,656 --> 00:10:31,706
something special happens right around one value.

198
00:10:31,706 --> 00:10:35,431
From z alone, a linear layer can only tilt a line;

199
00:10:35,431 --> 00:10:38,193
carving out a bump takes many layers of work.

200
00:10:38,193 --> 00:10:42,168
But a plain weighted sum of the sines and cosines already traces the bump

201
00:10:42,168 --> 00:10:43,281
closely.

202
00:10:43,281 --> 00:10:46,806
The features hand the network the building blocks of sharp effects, from the

203
00:10:46,806 --> 00:10:49,618
very first layer.

204
00:10:49,727 --> 00:10:52,214
Periodic features have one weakness.

205
00:10:52,214 --> 00:10:55,539
Outside the range seen in training, they simply repeat.

206
00:10:55,539 --> 00:10:59,152
A sine wave has no idea that five is bigger than one.

207
00:10:59,152 --> 00:11:01,452
So relflow keeps one more lane.

208
00:11:01,452 --> 00:11:05,989
The very last coordinate of a Number's vector is z itself, untouched:

209
00:11:05,989 --> 00:11:10,414
a straight ruler for scale, which later layers can add and multiply, even

210
00:11:10,414 --> 00:11:13,952
past the values they were trained on.

211
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:17,960
Put together, a Number's token is the vector for its state, plus a learned

212
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:21,685
projection of the Fourier features through a GELU, plus a straight line in

213
00:11:21,685 --> 00:11:25,085
z, with the last lane set to z itself.

214
00:11:25,085 --> 00:11:26,523
One safety rail:

215
00:11:26,523 --> 00:11:30,985
a value so extreme that the fastest sine would pass ten thousand radians,

216
00:11:30,985 --> 00:11:35,023
about 199 standard deviations out, gets clamped.

217
00:11:35,023 --> 00:11:37,360
Decoding runs the other way.

218
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,635
A Number's decoder predicts a state and a value, and its error is measured

219
00:11:41,635 --> 00:11:46,423
in standard deviations, so a field in dollars and a field in days pull on

220
00:11:46,423 --> 00:11:47,660
the model equally.

221
00:11:52,767 --> 00:11:54,367
Categories are labels:

222
00:11:54,367 --> 00:11:56,717
SKUs, merchants, countries.

223
00:11:56,717 --> 00:12:00,967
The classic recipe is to scan the whole dataset first, collect every label,

224
00:12:00,967 --> 00:12:04,505
and allocate an embedding table of exactly the right size.

225
00:12:04,505 --> 00:12:07,330
relflow streams, so it can't scan first.

226
00:12:07,330 --> 00:12:11,430
Instead, every Category field learns its vocabulary online.

227
00:12:11,430 --> 00:12:15,117
The first time a training batch contains a new label, that label gets the

228
00:12:15,117 --> 00:12:16,467
next free row.

229
00:12:16,467 --> 00:12:20,917
Labels are never evicted and never renumbered.

230
00:12:21,042 --> 00:12:22,430
There's a subtlety.

231
00:12:22,430 --> 00:12:26,742
Batches are encoded in data-loader worker processes, and a worker can't touch

232
00:12:26,742 --> 00:12:27,792
the model.

233
00:12:27,792 --> 00:12:31,817
So a worker numbers labels with a dictionary that's local to its own batch,

234
00:12:31,817 --> 00:12:34,667
like the zero to four we saw earlier.

235
00:12:34,667 --> 00:12:39,192
Then, in the training process, just before the forward pass, relflow binds

236
00:12:39,192 --> 00:12:43,667
those local numbers to the model's vocabulary, admits any new labels, and

237
00:12:43,667 --> 00:12:46,017
remaps the batch's tensors.

238
00:12:46,017 --> 00:12:51,205
With several GPUs, every rank gathers the batch's new labels in rank order,

239
00:12:51,205 --> 00:12:55,967
so all of them admit the same labels in the same order.

240
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:58,863
And when the table runs out of rows, it grows.

241
00:12:58,863 --> 00:13:02,563
Here's a real training run in which new merchants keep showing up, three more

242
00:13:02,563 --> 00:13:03,813
in every batch.

243
00:13:03,813 --> 00:13:06,125
The white dots count the labels admitted.

244
00:13:06,125 --> 00:13:08,713
The orange staircase is the storage behind them:

245
00:13:08,713 --> 00:13:13,013
one row, then four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four.

246
00:13:13,013 --> 00:13:17,388
Each time, the capacity jumps to the next power of two that holds every label,

247
00:13:17,388 --> 00:13:21,650
so growing is rare, and cheap on average.

248
00:13:21,809 --> 00:13:23,871
The tricky part is the optimizer.

249
00:13:23,871 --> 00:13:27,234
Adam keeps two running averages for every single parameter:

250
00:13:27,234 --> 00:13:30,159
one of its gradient, and one of the gradient squared.

251
00:13:30,159 --> 00:13:34,246
Growing the table means replacing the parameter with a bigger one, and a naive

252
00:13:34,246 --> 00:13:36,709
swap would throw that history away.

253
00:13:36,709 --> 00:13:41,021
relflow copies the old rows, their pending gradients, and Adam's moments into

254
00:13:41,021 --> 00:13:45,059
the new storage, and starts the new rows with zero moments, as if they had

255
00:13:45,059 --> 00:13:46,409
just been created.

256
00:13:46,409 --> 00:13:49,234
We checked this inside the run you just saw.

257
00:13:49,234 --> 00:13:53,384
At every growth, the old rows and their moments came through identical, bit

258
00:13:53,384 --> 00:13:57,909
for bit, and the new moments were exactly zero.

259
00:13:58,092 --> 00:14:00,205
Only training admits labels.

260
00:14:00,205 --> 00:14:04,717
Validation and testing reuse the mapping, so a label they've never seen stays

261
00:14:04,717 --> 00:14:09,505
unknown, and a model being served never learns anything from its requests.

262
00:14:09,505 --> 00:14:13,455
An unknown label is still valued, with zero content.

263
00:14:13,455 --> 00:14:17,617
To prepare the model for that, relflow hides one percent of known labels during

264
00:14:17,617 --> 00:14:21,992
training, by default, so the model learns to lean on the rest of the context

265
00:14:21,992 --> 00:14:23,592
when a label tells it nothing.

266
00:14:28,456 --> 00:14:30,494
Some values are identifiers:

267
00:14:30,494 --> 00:14:33,856
account numbers, device IDs, card numbers.

268
00:14:33,856 --> 00:14:37,419
There can be millions of them, and most show up once or twice.

269
00:14:37,419 --> 00:14:41,281
A vocabulary row for each one would memorize noise, and would know nothing

270
00:14:41,281 --> 00:14:43,831
about the new IDs arriving tomorrow.

271
00:14:43,831 --> 00:14:48,056
Often, what matters isn't which ID it is at all, only whether two of them

272
00:14:48,056 --> 00:14:49,131
are the same.

273
00:14:49,131 --> 00:14:52,881
Did this account just send money to itself?

274
00:14:53,056 --> 00:14:56,769
relflow's Hash type keeps exactly that, and nothing more.

275
00:14:56,769 --> 00:15:01,881
Each ID is hashed to 64-bit integers, in a few independent lanes, scaled to

276
00:15:01,881 --> 00:15:04,194
lie between minus one and one.

277
00:15:04,194 --> 00:15:08,494
Then the lanes go through sines and cosines at fixed frequencies, straight

278
00:15:08,494 --> 00:15:10,119
into model space.

279
00:15:10,119 --> 00:15:12,031
There's no learned table at all:

280
00:15:12,031 --> 00:15:15,781
the vector is a pure function of the hash.

281
00:15:15,981 --> 00:15:16,994
Now the trick.

282
00:15:16,994 --> 00:15:21,231
During training, every batch mixes a fresh random salt into the hash, so the

283
00:15:21,231 --> 00:15:25,369
very same account comes out as a completely different vector in the next batch.

284
00:15:25,369 --> 00:15:28,056
The model has no stable vector to memorize.

285
00:15:28,056 --> 00:15:32,756
But inside one batch, equal IDs always produce identical vectors, in every

286
00:15:32,756 --> 00:15:34,381
Hash field at once.

287
00:15:34,381 --> 00:15:38,306
Here are the similarities between the seven IDs of one observation, in three

288
00:15:38,306 --> 00:15:39,369
batches.

289
00:15:39,369 --> 00:15:41,306
The vectors change every time;

290
00:15:41,306 --> 00:15:43,981
the pattern of equalities never does.

291
00:15:43,981 --> 00:15:50,131
At test and prediction time the salt is zero, so serving is deterministic.

292
00:15:50,265 --> 00:15:51,127
Does it work?

293
00:15:51,127 --> 00:15:55,865
In relflow's proof P021, the answer depends only on whether two IDs match,

294
00:15:55,865 --> 00:15:58,202
and every test ID is brand new.

295
00:15:58,202 --> 00:16:01,352
Hash scores an AUC of 0.9999.

296
00:16:01,352 --> 00:16:05,340
A Category vocabulary, facing IDs it has never seen, can only guess:

297
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:06,290
one half.

298
00:16:06,290 --> 00:16:10,052
But equality only helps while the tokens can still attend to each other.

299
00:16:10,052 --> 00:16:14,390
In proof P028, two sibling collections are each summarized first, and then

300
00:16:14,390 --> 00:16:15,427
compared.

301
00:16:15,427 --> 00:16:16,777
The signal is gone:

302
00:16:16,777 --> 00:16:18,040
chance.

303
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:21,140
relflow records that one as a limitation.

304
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:25,965
It's why the documentation's Hash example keeps its transfers unsummarized,

305
00:16:25,965 --> 00:16:27,690
with reduction set to None.

306
00:16:32,505 --> 00:16:33,667
We have tokens now:

307
00:16:33,667 --> 00:16:35,992
one vector per field, per slot.

308
00:16:35,992 --> 00:16:39,830
Let's step inside the line items branch, and see what its encoder does with

309
00:16:39,830 --> 00:16:40,705
them.

310
00:16:40,705 --> 00:16:42,617
Arrange the tokens in a grid:

311
00:16:42,617 --> 00:16:45,617
one row per field, one column per slot.

312
00:16:45,617 --> 00:16:50,038
This order has five items, so the last three columns are padding.

313
00:16:50,038 --> 00:16:51,688
The first step is local.

314
00:16:51,688 --> 00:16:56,438
The SKU, the quantity and the price of one line item belong together.

315
00:16:56,438 --> 00:17:00,951
So each column, separately, goes through one small transformer layer:

316
00:17:00,951 --> 00:17:04,888
a sequence of just three tokens, attending only to each other.

317
00:17:04,888 --> 00:17:09,588
Afterwards, the SKU token knows its quantity, and the quantity token knows

318
00:17:09,588 --> 00:17:12,738
which product it's counting.

319
00:17:12,938 --> 00:17:16,201
Next, the grid is unrolled into a single sequence:

320
00:17:16,201 --> 00:17:21,501
all eight SKU slots, then all eight quantities, then all eight prices.

321
00:17:21,501 --> 00:17:23,201
Twenty-four positions.

322
00:17:23,201 --> 00:17:28,038
A stack of ordinary transformer layers runs across that whole sequence, so

323
00:17:28,038 --> 00:17:33,363
now every item can compare itself with every other item in the order.

324
00:17:33,497 --> 00:17:35,472
How does a token know where it sits?

325
00:17:35,472 --> 00:17:37,709
With rotary position embeddings.

326
00:17:37,709 --> 00:17:42,447
Every query and every key is rotated by an angle that grows with its position.

327
00:17:42,447 --> 00:17:46,822
Rotate both, and the score between two tokens depends only on how far apart

328
00:17:46,822 --> 00:17:47,734
they are.

329
00:17:47,734 --> 00:17:51,984
Here are real scores from relflow's rotary module, for one query and one key

330
00:17:51,984 --> 00:17:54,209
placed at every pair of positions:

331
00:17:54,209 --> 00:17:56,322
every diagonal is constant.

332
00:17:56,322 --> 00:17:59,172
And look what that buys inside a branch.

333
00:17:59,172 --> 00:18:04,322
An item's SKU and its own quantity are always exactly eight positions apart,

334
00:18:04,322 --> 00:18:06,034
whichever item it is.

335
00:18:06,034 --> 00:18:11,697
So 'my own quantity' is one fixed offset, the same for every item, and attention

336
00:18:11,697 --> 00:18:14,372
can learn it once.

337
00:18:14,555 --> 00:18:17,567
Each of those layers is the standard pre-norm block:

338
00:18:17,567 --> 00:18:20,342
normalize, attend, add the result back;

339
00:18:20,342 --> 00:18:22,967
normalize, feed forward, add back.

340
00:18:22,967 --> 00:18:26,642
Padding is zeroed on the way in and on the way out, and it's masked out of

341
00:18:26,642 --> 00:18:28,167
every attention.

342
00:18:28,167 --> 00:18:31,967
Each branch can also choose how many key and value heads to keep:

343
00:18:31,967 --> 00:18:38,405
one per query head, one per pair, one shared by all, or no attention at all.

344
00:18:38,563 --> 00:18:40,013
Does that pairing really matter?

345
00:18:40,013 --> 00:18:41,413
Here's a real test.

346
00:18:41,413 --> 00:18:45,213
We trained a small relflow model on synthetic orders, where an order comes

347
00:18:45,213 --> 00:18:49,738
back only if it contains a fragile SKU, bought three or more at a time.

348
00:18:49,738 --> 00:18:54,251
So S11 times four, next to S07 times one, comes back.

349
00:18:54,251 --> 00:18:56,263
Swap the quantities, and it doesn't.

350
00:18:56,263 --> 00:18:58,438
On new orders, the model is perfect:

351
00:18:58,438 --> 00:19:00,113
an AUC of one.

352
00:19:00,113 --> 00:19:03,888
Now take the same test orders and shuffle the quantities among each order's

353
00:19:03,888 --> 00:19:05,238
items.

354
00:19:05,238 --> 00:19:08,688
Same SKUs, same quantities, wrong pairs.

355
00:19:08,688 --> 00:19:11,626
The AUC drops to 0.91.

356
00:19:11,626 --> 00:19:15,338
The model wasn't counting SKUs and quantities separately.

357
00:19:15,338 --> 00:19:17,088
It was reading them as pairs.

358
00:19:22,003 --> 00:19:24,678
Padding costs the model's answers nothing.

359
00:19:24,678 --> 00:19:26,565
It isn't free for the hardware.

360
00:19:26,565 --> 00:19:28,615
A dense batch is a rectangle:

361
00:19:28,615 --> 00:19:33,453
every row is as long as the branch's capacity, whether it's full or not.

362
00:19:33,453 --> 00:19:37,590
And attention compares every pair of positions, so its cost grows with the

363
00:19:37,590 --> 00:19:39,278
square of that length.

364
00:19:39,278 --> 00:19:43,340
In this small real batch, 72 percent of the attention work would be spent

365
00:19:43,340 --> 00:19:45,728
on padding.

366
00:19:45,886 --> 00:19:49,236
So whenever some slots are empty, relflow packs.

367
00:19:49,236 --> 00:19:53,111
It gathers the live tokens into one flat list, remembering where each one

368
00:19:53,111 --> 00:19:54,174
came from.

369
00:19:54,174 --> 00:19:57,461
Then it groups the rows by how many live tokens they have:

370
00:19:57,461 --> 00:20:01,636
the three rows with one item together, the two rows with three items together,

371
00:20:01,636 --> 00:20:02,986
and so on.

372
00:20:02,986 --> 00:20:07,386
Inside a group every row has the same length, so attention runs on a perfect

373
00:20:07,386 --> 00:20:11,486
rectangle again, with no mask and no padding at all.

374
00:20:11,486 --> 00:20:15,786
Afterwards, every token is scattered back to its own slot, and the padding

375
00:20:15,786 --> 00:20:17,849
is restored as zeros.

376
00:20:17,849 --> 00:20:22,149
The rotary positions are the original ones, so the result is the same as the

377
00:20:22,149 --> 00:20:23,861
dense computation.

378
00:20:23,861 --> 00:20:24,861
We checked:

379
00:20:24,861 --> 00:20:30,811
the largest difference is about one part in a million, floating-point rounding.

380
00:20:30,961 --> 00:20:32,149
And it's fast.

381
00:20:32,149 --> 00:20:35,524
Here's relflow's own packing code, timed on this machine:

382
00:20:35,524 --> 00:20:41,211
two encoder layers over 256 rows, with room for 128 tokens each.

383
00:20:41,211 --> 00:20:46,124
When rows hold about four live tokens on average, dense attention takes 248

384
00:20:46,124 --> 00:20:47,124
milliseconds;

385
00:20:47,124 --> 00:20:48,661
packed, 14.

386
00:20:48,661 --> 00:20:50,661
Seventeen times faster.

387
00:20:50,661 --> 00:20:54,861
At twelve tokens it's six times faster, and even at thirty-two, a quarter

388
00:20:54,861 --> 00:20:58,261
full, packing halves the time.

389
00:20:58,395 --> 00:20:59,907
A little fine print.

390
00:20:59,907 --> 00:21:04,270
Grouping needs one trip from the GPU back to the host per encoder stack, to

391
00:21:04,270 --> 00:21:05,532
read the lengths.

392
00:21:05,532 --> 00:21:09,632
Packing steps aside when dropout is active, so the random numbers match the

393
00:21:09,632 --> 00:21:14,245
dense schedule exactly, and when anyone has hooked into or replaced the modules,

394
00:21:14,245 --> 00:21:17,295
so they keep seeing the dense shapes they expect.

395
00:21:17,295 --> 00:21:21,420
And rows with nothing in them at all are skipped entirely, everywhere.

396
00:21:26,318 --> 00:21:30,255
After its encoder, a branch still holds one token per field and slot:

397
00:21:30,255 --> 00:21:32,318
twenty-four of them, for our order.

398
00:21:32,318 --> 00:21:34,143
The parent doesn't want twenty-four.

399
00:21:34,143 --> 00:21:35,618
It wants a summary.

400
00:21:35,618 --> 00:21:39,630
relflow's default reduction is cross-attention from learned queries:

401
00:21:39,630 --> 00:21:43,780
a few vectors, trained like any other weights, each of which asks the collection

402
00:21:43,780 --> 00:21:47,743
a question, and comes back with a weighted average of the answers.

403
00:21:47,743 --> 00:21:51,168
One summary vector per query.

404
00:21:51,318 --> 00:21:53,118
Look closely at those weights.

405
00:21:53,118 --> 00:21:56,830
Softmax makes every weight positive, and makes them add up to one.

406
00:21:56,830 --> 00:21:59,618
So the output is always an average of the values.

407
00:21:59,618 --> 00:22:01,568
And averages have a blind spot:

408
00:22:01,568 --> 00:22:02,993
they can't count.

409
00:22:02,993 --> 00:22:06,630
One token carrying 1.25 gets all the weight.

410
00:22:06,630 --> 00:22:11,430
Six identical tokens split it, a sixth each, and add back up to exactly the

411
00:22:11,430 --> 00:22:12,705
same output.

412
00:22:12,705 --> 00:22:17,418
To the parent, one item and six items look identical.

413
00:22:17,560 --> 00:22:19,497
Let's watch that happen, for real.

414
00:22:19,497 --> 00:22:22,947
Each training record holds a bag of identical amounts, between one and six

415
00:22:22,947 --> 00:22:25,497
of them, and the hidden target is their total.

416
00:22:25,497 --> 00:22:29,597
Branch attention is switched off, so only the reduction can do the counting.

417
00:22:29,597 --> 00:22:34,135
Then we ask each trained model about one copy of 1.25, two copies, and so

418
00:22:34,135 --> 00:22:35,485
on, up to ten.

419
00:22:35,485 --> 00:22:38,635
First, the plain average reduction, rf dot Mean.

420
00:22:38,635 --> 00:22:41,535
It answers 4.39, every time.

421
00:22:41,535 --> 00:22:45,310
Next, attention, with two of relflow's lanes removed.

422
00:22:45,310 --> 00:22:47,960
4.22, every time.

423
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:51,947
Both have learned to guess about three and a half times the amount, the average

424
00:22:51,947 --> 00:22:56,551
count, because the count itself never reaches them.

425
00:22:56,551 --> 00:23:00,964
So relflow's reduction adds two lanes that softmax can't provide.

426
00:23:00,964 --> 00:23:02,501
The first is mass:

427
00:23:02,501 --> 00:23:07,514
the sum of the branch's input tokens, a sum, not an average, scaled down by

428
00:23:07,514 --> 00:23:12,089
the square root of the branch's capacity, and sent through a learned projection.

429
00:23:12,089 --> 00:23:13,776
The second is a count:

430
00:23:13,776 --> 00:23:19,126
the number of present tokens, divided by the capacity, times a learned direction.

431
00:23:19,126 --> 00:23:24,164
Both are added to every summary vector, and both start at exactly zero, so

432
00:23:24,164 --> 00:23:28,551
the reduction begins life as pure attention and learns how much of each lane

433
00:23:28,551 --> 00:23:30,768
to use.

434
00:23:30,768 --> 00:23:34,180
And with those lanes switched on, the same model counts.

435
00:23:34,180 --> 00:23:41,105
1.26, 2.47, 3.70, all the way to 7.44 for six copies:

436
00:23:41,105 --> 00:23:44,368
right on the line, across the whole range it was trained on.

437
00:23:44,368 --> 00:23:49,755
Its typical error on new bags drops from about 1.7 to 0.07.

438
00:23:49,755 --> 00:23:53,243
Past six copies, though, our model flattens out.

439
00:23:53,243 --> 00:23:58,693
relflow's own proof of this, P002, with a different setup, does stretch somewhat

440
00:23:58,693 --> 00:24:03,318
beyond its training lengths, and calls that a bounded extension, not exact

441
00:24:03,318 --> 00:24:04,655
arithmetic.

442
00:24:04,655 --> 00:24:07,005
The documentation says it plainly:

443
00:24:07,005 --> 00:24:12,168
if you need an exact business total, compute it in preprocessing.

444
00:24:12,351 --> 00:24:15,251
So a branch has three ways to report to its parent.

445
00:24:15,251 --> 00:24:16,176
The default:

446
00:24:16,176 --> 00:24:20,014
a fixed number of learned summaries, with the mass and count lanes.

447
00:24:20,014 --> 00:24:21,264
rf dot Mean:

448
00:24:21,264 --> 00:24:22,851
one plain average.

449
00:24:22,851 --> 00:24:25,989
Cheap, but as we've just seen, it forgets how many.

450
00:24:25,989 --> 00:24:30,664
Or reduction equals None, which passes every encoded slot straight up, so

451
00:24:30,664 --> 00:24:34,551
items stay distinct, at the price of a longer sequence in the parent.

452
00:24:39,441 --> 00:24:42,791
Now let's run the whole customer model and follow the shapes, from the leaves

453
00:24:42,791 --> 00:24:43,578
up.

454
00:24:43,578 --> 00:24:47,703
Every field has been embedded into vectors of width 256.

455
00:24:47,703 --> 00:24:49,666
The deepest branch runs first.

456
00:24:49,666 --> 00:24:55,428
For each of a customer's 64 orders, the line items encoder sees 16 SKU slots

457
00:24:55,428 --> 00:24:57,328
and 16 quantity slots:

458
00:24:57,328 --> 00:24:58,966
32 tokens.

459
00:24:58,966 --> 00:25:03,691
It encodes them, and reduces them to four summary vectors, the medium preset's

460
00:25:03,691 --> 00:25:05,416
number of outputs.

461
00:25:05,416 --> 00:25:09,891
All 64 orders are processed at once, as one big batch.

462
00:25:09,891 --> 00:25:13,791
Those four vectors are routed up into the orders branch, as if they were four

463
00:25:13,791 --> 00:25:15,641
more fields of each order.

464
00:25:15,641 --> 00:25:19,953
So every order contributes its age, plus four line-item summaries:

465
00:25:19,953 --> 00:25:25,003
five tokens, times 64 orders, is 320 tokens.

466
00:25:25,003 --> 00:25:29,303
The orders encoder runs across all of them, so an order can be compared with

467
00:25:29,303 --> 00:25:34,016
every other order, and reduces them to four vectors per customer.

468
00:25:34,016 --> 00:25:35,541
Finally, the root.

469
00:25:35,541 --> 00:25:39,553
Its sequence is the tenure, the four order summaries, and a slot for each

470
00:25:39,553 --> 00:25:42,966
of the two targets, which stay empty, because they're masked:

471
00:25:42,966 --> 00:25:44,641
seven slots in all.

472
00:25:44,641 --> 00:25:48,991
Three layers of attention, the deepest stack in the model, and a last reduction

473
00:25:48,991 --> 00:25:52,024
gives the customer's four root vectors.

474
00:25:52,024 --> 00:25:54,337
That's relflow's execution plan:

475
00:25:54,337 --> 00:25:58,787
branches grouped by depth and run deepest first, each one waiting only for

476
00:25:58,787 --> 00:26:00,224
its own children.

477
00:26:00,224 --> 00:26:03,374
And every hand-off between levels is just a reshape:

478
00:26:03,374 --> 00:26:08,737
the item axis folds into the order axis, and the order axis into the customer.

479
00:26:08,737 --> 00:26:11,599
Nothing is copied out to Python along the way.

480
00:26:16,581 --> 00:26:18,919
So far everything has flowed up the tree.

481
00:26:18,919 --> 00:26:20,944
Decoders read it back down.

482
00:26:20,944 --> 00:26:24,781
Each target's decoder looks at the representations along its own path to the

483
00:26:24,781 --> 00:26:25,494
root:

484
00:26:25,494 --> 00:26:26,731
its heritage.

485
00:26:26,731 --> 00:26:29,794
For churned, that's simply the root's four vectors.

486
00:26:29,794 --> 00:26:34,606
For a field deep in the tree, like a line item's SKU, it's the root, the order

487
00:26:34,606 --> 00:26:40,481
summaries, the item summaries of every order, and the field's own slots.

488
00:26:40,598 --> 00:26:43,023
The decoder doesn't average its heritage.

489
00:26:43,023 --> 00:26:47,423
It lays it out as one long memory, and it brings learned queries of its own,

490
00:26:47,423 --> 00:26:48,961
shaped like the target:

491
00:26:48,961 --> 00:26:54,373
a single query for churned, or one for each of the 64 times 16 item slots,

492
00:26:54,373 --> 00:26:55,311
for SKU.

493
00:26:55,311 --> 00:26:59,073
Each query cross-attends into the memory and pulls out what it needs.

494
00:26:59,073 --> 00:27:03,173
And before it does, each query is nudged by the visible siblings at its own

495
00:27:03,173 --> 00:27:04,573
coordinate.

496
00:27:04,573 --> 00:27:09,311
When the decoder predicts an item's SKU, the query for that slot starts out

497
00:27:09,311 --> 00:27:12,398
knowing the item's quantity.

498
00:27:12,523 --> 00:27:14,811
Then a small head for each datatype.

499
00:27:14,811 --> 00:27:18,873
Every decoder predicts the five-way state, so the model can say that a value

500
00:27:18,873 --> 00:27:20,111
will be null.

501
00:27:20,111 --> 00:27:24,898
A Number adds a value, a Boolean a probability, and a Category a distribution

502
00:27:24,898 --> 00:27:27,711
over the labels it has discovered so far.

503
00:27:27,711 --> 00:27:32,286
The loss adds the state's cross-entropy and the content's own loss, counted

504
00:27:32,286 --> 00:27:36,348
only on the coordinates selected for reconstruction, and each field's total

505
00:27:36,348 --> 00:27:38,773
can be weighted.

506
00:27:38,931 --> 00:27:40,769
Which coordinates get selected?

507
00:27:40,769 --> 00:27:44,569
That's the job of masks, one mechanism with several uses.

508
00:27:44,569 --> 00:27:47,181
mask equals True makes a supervised target:

509
00:27:47,181 --> 00:27:50,331
the value is never embedded, and always reconstructed.

510
00:27:50,331 --> 00:27:53,894
mask equals 0.15 is training-only dropout:

511
00:27:53,894 --> 00:27:57,694
fifteen percent of values hide behind a learned mask token.

512
00:27:57,694 --> 00:28:02,419
A Mask with a rate and reconstruct equals True is self-supervised learning:

513
00:28:02,419 --> 00:28:05,906
hide a sample, and learn to predict it from the rest.

514
00:28:05,906 --> 00:28:10,519
A Mask with a query lets a Boolean column in the data decide, and a mask on

515
00:28:10,519 --> 00:28:15,306
a branch makes one decision for its entire subtree.

516
00:28:15,506 --> 00:28:17,594
Sampling is deterministic.

517
00:28:17,594 --> 00:28:21,831
Every coordinate gets a score from a random generator seeded by a SHA-256

518
00:28:21,831 --> 00:28:26,694
hash of the seed, the stage, the epoch, the field's address and the policy.

519
00:28:26,694 --> 00:28:30,894
Here are real selections at a fifteen percent rate, on a batch of orders.

520
00:28:30,894 --> 00:28:34,419
Epoch zero picks the same coordinates every time you run it.

521
00:28:34,419 --> 00:28:36,556
Epoch one picks fresh ones.

522
00:28:36,556 --> 00:28:40,944
And validation always pretends it's epoch zero, so a validation score means

523
00:28:40,944 --> 00:28:44,756
the same thing from one epoch to the next.

524
00:28:44,890 --> 00:28:46,565
And the stages differ.

525
00:28:46,565 --> 00:28:49,127
Dropout only acts during training.

526
00:28:49,127 --> 00:28:51,427
A fixed target is hidden everywhere:

527
00:28:51,427 --> 00:28:53,290
that's what makes it a prediction.

528
00:28:53,290 --> 00:28:58,240
And a sampled reconstruction trains its decoder, and is measured during validation,

529
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:01,265
but asks for no predictions when the model is serving.

530
00:29:06,146 --> 00:29:08,884
Let's finish by watching a real model learn.

531
00:29:08,884 --> 00:29:10,209
Here's the schema:

532
00:29:10,209 --> 00:29:14,871
orders with up to eight line items, each with a SKU, a quantity and a price,

533
00:29:14,871 --> 00:29:16,471
and one hidden Boolean:

534
00:29:16,471 --> 00:29:18,096
was the order returned?

535
00:29:18,096 --> 00:29:20,496
The rule, which the model never sees:

536
00:29:20,496 --> 00:29:26,284
an order comes back if it contains a fragile SKU, S03 or S11, bought three

537
00:29:26,284 --> 00:29:28,971
or more at a time.

538
00:29:29,142 --> 00:29:33,830
We train on four thousand synthetic orders, on this machine's processor.

539
00:29:33,830 --> 00:29:36,255
The whole run takes about 10 seconds.

540
00:29:36,255 --> 00:29:41,042
The loss falls fast, and by the end of the second epoch, validation AUC has

541
00:29:41,042 --> 00:29:43,317
reached one, and it stays there.

542
00:29:43,317 --> 00:29:48,467
On two thousand fresh test orders, it doesn't miss a single one.

543
00:29:48,601 --> 00:29:49,951
But how does it decide?

544
00:29:49,951 --> 00:29:53,363
We can open up the line items branch, and recompute the attention weights

545
00:29:53,363 --> 00:29:55,463
of its learned summary query.

546
00:29:55,463 --> 00:29:57,388
Here's an order that came back:

547
00:29:57,388 --> 00:30:00,676
S11, four at a time, in the fifth slot.

548
00:30:00,676 --> 00:30:04,976
Two of the four heads put essentially all of their weight on one single token:

549
00:30:04,976 --> 00:30:07,726
the SKU of exactly that item.

550
00:30:07,726 --> 00:30:13,476
And thanks to coordinate attention, that SKU token already knows its own quantity.

551
00:30:13,476 --> 00:30:17,501
Here's a second returned order, with the fragile item in the first slot:

552
00:30:17,501 --> 00:30:21,026
the same two heads go straight to it.

553
00:30:21,159 --> 00:30:23,446
And here are two orders that were kept.

554
00:30:23,446 --> 00:30:27,496
Each one contains a fragile SKU, but bought only once.

555
00:30:27,496 --> 00:30:31,909
There's no single decisive item, and the attention spreads out and wanders:

556
00:30:31,909 --> 00:30:34,371
over quantities, over other products.

557
00:30:34,371 --> 00:30:39,309
The model says no, with 99 percent confidence.

558
00:30:39,451 --> 00:30:44,113
Because we set embed equals True, the model also exports its root vector for

559
00:30:44,113 --> 00:30:45,438
every order.

560
00:30:45,438 --> 00:30:50,151
Project six hundred test orders onto their two main directions, and the returned

561
00:30:50,151 --> 00:30:55,226
orders sit cleanly apart from the kept ones, along the very first direction.

562
00:30:55,226 --> 00:30:59,426
That vector is what a downstream model, or a nearest-neighbor search, would

563
00:30:59,426 --> 00:31:01,626
use.

564
00:31:01,780 --> 00:31:04,580
And predictions come back as an Arrow table.

565
00:31:04,580 --> 00:31:05,955
For every address:

566
00:31:05,955 --> 00:31:10,417
a probability for each state, the decoded content, and a flag saying whether

567
00:31:10,417 --> 00:31:12,217
the value was inferred.

568
00:31:12,217 --> 00:31:15,117
Exported embeddings sit at their own addresses.

569
00:31:15,117 --> 00:31:19,380
In a batch job, a writer callback streams each batch of predictions straight

570
00:31:19,380 --> 00:31:22,255
to Parquet, so nothing piles up in memory.

571
00:31:27,019 --> 00:31:29,807
How do we know any of this works, beyond a few demos?

572
00:31:29,807 --> 00:31:31,907
relflow ships its own evidence:

573
00:31:31,907 --> 00:31:36,369
a catalog of 78 proofs, small controlled experiments that each test one learning

574
00:31:36,369 --> 00:31:39,857
claim, with controls designed to fail when the claim is false.

575
00:31:39,857 --> 00:31:44,257
They're empirical tests, not mathematical proofs, and the catalog says so.

576
00:31:44,257 --> 00:31:48,382
55 are marked passing, 13 limited, and 10 partial.

577
00:31:48,382 --> 00:31:52,119
In the latest recorded runs, seven of them missed their own gates, and that's

578
00:31:52,119 --> 00:31:54,694
recorded too.

579
00:31:54,819 --> 00:31:56,232
A few highlights.

580
00:31:56,232 --> 00:31:58,219
Counts can survive a hierarchy:

581
00:31:58,219 --> 00:32:02,369
six equal amounts, grouped into sessions in different ways, and the model

582
00:32:02,369 --> 00:32:06,407
recovers ninety-one percent of the true difference between the groupings.

583
00:32:06,407 --> 00:32:09,032
Reconstruction can spot contradictions:

584
00:32:09,032 --> 00:32:13,057
records where every field looks normal on its own, but the combination is

585
00:32:13,057 --> 00:32:19,619
wrong, get flagged with an AUC of 0.96, where any single field gives chance.

586
00:32:19,619 --> 00:32:21,919
And calendar features pick up phase:

587
00:32:21,919 --> 00:32:26,407
the month, from the day of the year, at 92 percent, against ten percent with

588
00:32:26,407 --> 00:32:29,069
the days shuffled.

589
00:32:29,219 --> 00:32:31,832
And the limits are recorded just as carefully.

590
00:32:31,832 --> 00:32:35,344
Comparing identities across two collections that have each been summarized

591
00:32:35,344 --> 00:32:37,282
fails, as we saw.

592
00:32:37,282 --> 00:32:41,594
Admitting a brand-new label in the middle of training activates a random row,

593
00:32:41,594 --> 00:32:45,419
which can jolt predictions before it has learned anything, and the obvious

594
00:32:45,419 --> 00:32:49,044
fixes made other proofs worse, so they weren't adopted.

595
00:32:49,044 --> 00:32:52,969
And combining two familiar instructions in a pairing never seen in training

596
00:32:52,969 --> 00:32:57,369
works on some seeds, but not reliably yet.

597
00:32:57,544 --> 00:32:59,519
So, the journey of one batch.

598
00:32:59,519 --> 00:33:03,244
Nested Arrow records are laid into fixed rectangles by a single placement

599
00:33:03,244 --> 00:33:06,107
rule, with a state beside every value.

600
00:33:06,107 --> 00:33:08,557
Each field becomes vectors in its own way:

601
00:33:08,557 --> 00:33:13,094
Fourier features and a ruler for numbers, a growing table for labels, a salted

602
00:33:13,094 --> 00:33:14,882
hash for identities.

603
00:33:14,882 --> 00:33:18,919
Each branch lets an item's fields talk, then lets its items talk, with the

604
00:33:18,919 --> 00:33:22,882
padding packed away, and reports to its parent with summaries that can still

605
00:33:22,882 --> 00:33:24,069
count.

606
00:33:24,069 --> 00:33:27,869
And decoders reach back down the tree, to fill in whatever the masks have

607
00:33:27,869 --> 00:33:29,128
hidden.

608
00:33:29,128 --> 00:33:31,490
The schema you write is the network you get.

609
00:33:31,490 --> 00:33:32,678
Thanks for watching.
